00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.160
Imagine you bought an energy ETF last week after oil spiked on geopolitical headlines

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:21.740
and your order filled at the market open. The chart showed price sitting just above the 50-day

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:29.340
moving average with RSI 14 at 58, yet the next two sessions erased the entire gain.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:36.460
What actually happened is that the 20-day average volume was only 60% of normal on the breakout day,

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.040
so the move lacked the institutional participation needed to hold the level.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:48.920
Moving averages, especially the 50-day and 200-day, act as dynamic support and resistance zones,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.960
rather than magic lines. A golden cross has shown positive forward returns and broad indices

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:02.620
over multi-year periods, but the same pattern on single stocks produces far more false signals.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:10.860
Volume confirmation remains the single most reliable filter. Rising price on above average volume

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:17.000
increases the odds the move is real, while declining volume on a rally is a classic warning.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:25.860
RSI above 70 or below 30 flags overbought or oversold conditions on the 14-day setting.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:33.100
Yet these readings frequently persist in strong trends. MACD crossovers add momentum

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:40.640
context, but lag in fast markets. The honest limitation is that technical analysis improves entry

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.460
and exit timing far more than it predicts which stocks will fundamentally outperform.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:52.460
Always require at least one confirming factor, such as volume surge, earnings catalyst,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.000
or sector rotation, before acting on a pure price pattern.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:05.720
Open your brokerage platform, pull up the 50-day and 200-day moving averages on one energy ETF

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.440
you already own or are watching, and note whether price is above or below both.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:19.580
If it sits above the 50-day, but below the 200-day, calculate the distance to the 200-day line as a

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:26.080
percentage. That is your potential mean reversion target if oil holds its current premium.

